{"name":"Les Cavaliers Beach","hex":"#0f63b3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"les","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Lese."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Pronunciation spelling of let's"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/les"},{"term":"cavaliers","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of cavalier"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of cavalier"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cavaliers"},{"term":"beach","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The shore of a body of water, especially when sandy or pebbly.","A horizontal strip of land, usually sandy, adjoining water."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To run aground on a beach.","To run (something) aground on a beach."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/beach"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cavalier","blocks":[{"text":"The term Cavalier was first used to describe cavalrymen, and later by Roundheads as a term of abuse for the wealthier royalist supporters of Charles I of England and his son Charles II during the English Civil War, the Interregnum, and the Restoration. It was later adopted by the Royalists themselves. Although it referred originally to political and social attitudes and behaviour, of which clothing was a very small part, it has subsequently become strongly identified with the fashionable clothing of the court at the time. Prince Rupert, commander of much of Charles I's cavalry, is often considered to be an archetypal Cavalier."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Anthonis_van_Dyck_058.jpg/330px-Anthonis_van_Dyck_058.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cavalier","wikidata":"Q2284765"},{"title":"Beach","blocks":[{"text":"A beach is a landform alongside a body of water which consists of loose particles. The particles composing a 